==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|peculiar|ly}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-peculiarly.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# Such as to be greater than usual; [[particularly]]; [[exceptionally]].
## {{lb|en|degree}} To greater degree than is usual.
##: {{ux|en|He has a '''peculiarly''' strong accent.}}
##: {{ux|en|This racehorse is '''peculiarly''' fast.}}
##* '''1916''', [[w:John Dewey|John Dewey]], ''[[s:Democracy and Education/Section 14|Democracy and Education]]'', Section 14.
##*: Democratic society is '''peculiarly''' dependent for its maintenance upon the use in forming a course of study of criteria which are broadly human.
##* '''1917''', [1877], [[w:Leo Tolstoy|Leo Tolstoy]], [[w:Constance Garnett|Constance Garnett]] (translator), ''[[s:Anna Karenina/Part One/Chapter 18|Anna Karenina]]'', Part One, Chapter 18,
##*: He begged pardon, and was getting into the carriage, but felt he must glance at her once more; not that she was very beautiful, not on account of the elegance and modest grace which were apparent in her whole figure, but because in the expression of her charming face, as she passed close by him, there was something '''peculiarly''' caressing and soft.
## {{lb|en|manner}} In a manner that is greater than usual.
##* '''1818''', [[w:Mary Shelley|Mary Shelley]], ''[[s:Frankenstein/Chapter 4|Frankenstein, or the Modern Prometheus]]'', Chapter 4,
##*: One of the phenomena which had '''peculiarly''' attracted my attention was the structure of the human frame, and, indeed, any animal endued with life.
##* '''1913''' July 3, [[w:Theodore Roosevelt|Theodore Roosevelt]], ''[[s:Address to the Boys Progressive League|Address to the Boys Progressive League]]'',
##*: I feel that the Progressive Party should appeal '''peculiarly''' to the young men--and therefore to the boys--who ought to be the next generation of voters.
# Such as to be [[strange]] or [[odd]].
## {{lb|en|degree}} [[strangely|Strangely]], [[oddly]].
##: {{ux|en|His nose is '''peculiarly''' bent.}}
## {{lb|en|manner}} In a [[strange]] or [[perverse]] manner; [[strangely]].
##: {{ux|en|He dresses '''peculiarly'''.}}
## {{lb|en|evaluative}} Such as to be [[strange]] or [[odd]].
##: {{ux|en|'''Peculiarly''', his hat is on upside-down.}}
## {{lb|en|act-related}} Acting in [[strange]] or [[perverse]] way.
##: {{ux|en|'''Peculiarly''', he left through the window.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Peculiarly''', he sat bolt upright and shouted "Geronimo!" whenever John Wayne appeared.}}
# Strongly associated with.
## {{lb|en|degree|of a place or circumstance}} Mostly or solely associated with.
##: {{ux|en|Having red hair and freckles is a characteristic that is '''peculiarly''' Northern European.}}
##* '''1907''', [[w:Swami Vivekananda|Swami Vivekananda]], ''[[s:The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda/Volume 6/Lectures and Discourses/The Methods and Purpose of Religion|The Methods and Purpose of Religion]]'', in ''[[s:The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda|The Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda]]'', Volume 6: Lectures and Discourses,
##*: This is one of the methods of procedure. The other is through man to God. The second is '''peculiarly''' Aryan, and the first is '''peculiarly''' Semitic.
##* '''1917''', [[w:Thomas Seltzer (translator)|Thomas Seltzer]] (translator), ''[[s:Best Russian Short Stories/Introduction|Best Russian Short Stories]]'', Introduction,
##*: However, he already gave strong indication of the '''peculiarly''' Russian genius for naturalness or realism, and was a true Russian in his simplicity of style.

====Synonyms====
